{
  "title": "I work, you work: present simple",
  "kind": "grammar",
  "cefr": "A1",
  "estMinutes": 8,
  "intro": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Form"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "header": true,
      "rows": [
        [
          "person",
          "verb"
        ],
        [
          "I",
          "work"
        ],
        [
          "you",
          "work"
        ],
        [
          "we",
          "work"
        ],
        [
          "they",
          "work"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "With I, you, we and they, the verb does not change. (He / she / it is the next lesson.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Use"
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "title": "We use the present simple for",
      "points": [
        "habits and routines: I drink coffee every morning.",
        "things that are true for a long time: We live in Tashkent.",
        "facts: Cats eat fish."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "title": "Time words",
      "points": [
        "every day / every week / every Monday",
        "in the morning / at night / at the weekend",
        "They usually go at the END of the sentence."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "en": "They study English every day."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3. Present simple or am / is / are?"
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "title": "Be and other verbs",
      "points": [
        "Use am / is / are with adjectives, nouns, places: I am tired. I am a student.",
        "Use the present simple for actions: I work. I study.",
        "Do not use both: I work (not I am work)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Be careful!"
    },
    {
      "type": "mistake",
      "wrong": "I am work in a bank.",
      "right": "I work in a bank."
    },
    {
      "type": "mistake",
      "wrong": "Every day I tea drink.",
      "right": "I drink tea every day."
    },
    {
      "type": "mistake",
      "wrong": "We lives in Samarkand.",
      "right": "We live in Samarkand."
    }
  ],
